[{"id": "question 7471", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "For redirecting a UE to another E-UTRA frequency, the eNB issues an RRC Connection Release message carrying the redirectedCarrierInfo IE which identifies the target frequency. The UE CONTEXT RELEASE REQUEST is an S1-AP message used to release S1 UE context at the MME/S-GW, not for inter-frequency redirection.", "source": "3GPP TS 36.331 RRCConnectionRelease with RedirectedCarrierInfo IE"}, {"id": "question 7481", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "In the canonical Client-Server model, the Server is the passive entity (listens for incoming connections) while the Client is the active initiator. The marked option 2 reverses the roles. Option 3 is the correct statement that the Server software can inform the application program when a request has arrived.", "source": "IETF RFC 793 / TCP/IP client-server architecture reference"}, {"id": "question 7515", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "VLR holds subscriber-related information needed for call handling in its served area. The marked option 4 (route calls to actual location) describes a function more accurately performed by the MSC+VLR combination, while option 1 mischaracterises VLR as handling 'packet data transfer' (a PS-side SGSN role). None of the options cleanly and uniquely captures VLR's primary local-data-store function.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.002 / TR 21.905 VLR definition"}, {"id": "question 7614", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Periodic network selection attempts in the UE factor in PLMN availability and priority lists (Requested NSSAI / HPLMN search / higher-priority PLMN search). The combination of PLMN priority and availability is the canonical criterion for periodic reselection.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.122 §4.4 (Periodic network selection)"}, {"id": "question 7998", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "The Common Control Channel (CCCH) is the logical channel used for UE/network control signalling before dedicated resources exist and for common uplink control during initial access. BCCH/PCCH are downlink-only broadcast/paging; DCCH is dedicated UE-specific control.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.300 §6.1.4 logical channel definitions"}, {"id": "question 8028", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Per TS 37.340 / TS 38.300 DC configuration for a DRB depends on bearer-type mapping, MCG/SCG membership, S-NSSAI/slice, and QoS profile elements. Multiple factors (option 1 RSN/PDU Session Pair ID and option 2 slice) interact; the marked 'network slice' is not unambiguously the sole determinant.", "source": "3GPP TS 37.340 DC architecture; TS 38.300"}, {"id": "question 8092", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "TS 25.401 §10.1.2: 'Logical O&M is the signalling associated with the control of logical resources (channels, cells,...) owned by the RNC but physically implemented in the Node B... All messages needed to support this information exchange are classified as Logical O&M.' The purpose is facilitating the RNC↔Node B information exchange related to logical resources.", "source": "3GPP TS 25.401 §10.1.2 (Logical O&M)"}, {"id": "question 8120", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "The defining mechanism of MPTCP for cross-network efficiency is coupled congestion control (RFC 6356, updated in RFC 8684), which ensures aggregate fairness against single-path TCP while shifting traffic toward less congested paths. Per-packet load balancing is one scheduling implementation; it is not the central enabling mechanism.", "source": "IETF RFC 8684 / RFC 6356 (MPTCP coupled congestion control)"}, {"id": "question 8265", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "The Transport Layer (OSI Layer 4; e.g., TCP) provides a reliable, logical point-to-point data transmission service between hosts. Data Link is hop-by-hop; Network is best-effort routing.", "source": "ISO/IEC 7498-1 OSI reference model"}, {"id": "question 8361", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "Default media-handling capability of IMS Multimedia Telephony per TS 26.114 / TS 22.173 is full-duplex speech and real-time text. Adding/removing individual media components is a content-manipulation feature, not the default basic media capability.", "source": "3GPP TS 26.114; TS 22.173 IMS Multimedia Telephony"}, {"id": "question 8364", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "NG-RAN positioning in TS 38.300 covers signalling support for positioning (e.g. NRPPa). Among the listed options, none directly names the positioning-support function; PCI/CCO/RACH Optimisation and NR MBS are SON/scheduling features not specifically named 'positioning' per the question wording.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.300 §9 NG-RAN functions"}, {"id": "question 8411", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "Access Class Barring (ACB) per TS 22.011 / TS 36.331 is the general access-control mechanism that applies at idle-mode access attempts, including CSFB attempts to establish an MO/MT fallback. SSAC is IMS-specific; EAB is an aggressive variant of ACB.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.011 Access Class Barring; TS 36.331"}, {"id": "question 8441", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "IEEE 802.11 open-system authentication is a two-frame identity exchange with no cryptographic verification: only the requesting station's identity (MAC) is communicated/accepted. Shared-key authentication is mutual; MAC filtering is not authentication proper.", "source": "IEEE 802.11-2020 §11.1.2 authentication types"}, {"id": "question 8500", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "Per RFC 2131 §3.1, DHCP Discover is a client broadcast that informs any available DHCP servers of the host's IP-data requirements and detects the existence of DHCP servers. The other options describe DHCP Request, Acknowledgement, or selection steps.", "source": "IETF RFC 2131 §3.1 (DHCP DISCOVER)"}, {"id": "question 8636", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Layer-2 measurements (TS 38.314 / TS 28.552) exist to support network optimization across RACH optimisation, load balancing, mobility robustness, and QoS verification. PRB-usage-for-MIMO is a specific measurement item, not the overarching purpose. The broader aim is mobility/network optimisation.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.314 Layer-2 measurements"}, {"id": "question 8745", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Fast Ring Detection is the well-known RSTP extension used in industrial Ethernet (e.g. Phoenix Contact, Hirschmann) to dramatically reduce ring-topology convergence time (100-500 ms) compared to plain RSTP.", "source": "IEEE 802.1D-2004 RSTP; Phoenix Contact / Hirschmann industrial Ethernet documentation"}, {"id": "question 8770", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per TS 23.502 §4.11.3 (and corroborated by vendor implementations), after an AMF purges subscriber data and MM context it issues Nudm_SDM_Unsubscribe to UDM to cancel its subscription to subscription-data change notifications. Option 2 matches this exactly.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.502 §4.11.3; Nudm_SDM_Unsubscribe"}, {"id": "question 8782", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "In the FMC/ATSSS architecture, RACFs (RAT-Agnostic Control Functions) interface with the Flow Controller (FC) for QoS enforcement and with the RSCFs (RAT-Specific Control Functions) for per-RAT policy.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.716 (FMC study); TS 23.501 ATSSS"}, {"id": "question 8798", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "'Inter-gNB-DU Conditional PSCell Change using MCG SRB without MN negotiation' enables low-latency PSCell move between gNB-DUs without MN involvement. The listed options are procedure steps/actions, not a purpose statement; none cleanly maps to 'purpose'.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.300 §9; TS 37.340 (CPC Rel-17/18)"}, {"id": "question 8808", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "Per 3GPP WLAN-interworking / PDG specs, the 'Wi' reference point connects the PDG to the external packet data network. -Wm connects the UE to the PDG; -Wd links the PDG to AAA.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.234 / TS 23.882 PDG reference points"}, {"id": "question 8833", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "Per TS 38.321 §5.7 and TS 38.331, when RRC has not configured a secondary DRX group, all serving cells belong to one DRX group - this is the default configuration. Option 3 captures this.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.7; TS 38.331 DRX-Config"}, {"id": "question 9013", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "Per TS 22.011 / TS 36.331, in connected mode the network controls UE behaviour via 'Prevention of mobile-originating signalling and/or data traffic'. ACB/EAB are idle-mode; SSAC is IMS-specific; ASCDC targets specific data applications.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.011; TS 36.331 connected-mode control"}, {"id": "question 9014", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per TS 29.520 §4.3.1.2 and TS 23.288, PCF is an explicitly listed consumer of 'Network Performance' analytics: 'PCF supports taking analytics information for network performance from the NWDAF.' PCF is the canonical cited consumer.", "source": "3GPP TS 29.520 §4.3.1.2; TS 23.288"}, {"id": "question 9050", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "For NWDAF data-delivery subscription the standard service is exposed via the Nnwdaf service-based interface (Nnwdaf_AnalyticsSubscription / Nnwdaf_AnalyticsInfo per TS 29.520). 'Nnf' is a generic service-based bus reference, not the specific NWDAF subscription interface.", "source": "3GPP TS 29.520 (Nnwdaf services)"}, {"id": "question 9183", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 21.905 / TS 43.022, the GERAN Base Station Subsystem (BSS) is responsible for establishing the means of connection between the MS and the GERAN. The other options narrow to BTS/BSC-specific functions rather than the BSS as a whole.", "source": "3GPP TS 21.905 (BSS definition); TS 43.022"}, {"id": "question 9237", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per TS 32.299 / TS 32.240, in online charging with reservation, if service-execution fails after units were reserved, the OCS refunds the unused reserved amount back to the subscriber account through the appropriate debit/refund procedure.", "source": "3GPP TS 32.299 (online charging); TS 32.240"}, {"id": "question 9277", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "When the network cannot provide the requested QoS, IMS signalling may inform the applications (e.g. reduced AVP/parameters), but the precise outcome (option 2 application-inform, option 1 reduced QoS, option 4 service refusal) depends on the bearer type and policy. None is unambiguously the only outcome.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.207; TS 23.228 (IMS QoS)"}, {"id": "question 9359", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per TS 23.288, NWDAF generates a defined set of specific (specialized) analytics types (slice load, NF load, network performance, UE mobility, etc.) identified by Analytics IDs. Only these specialized types are defined; general 'any' analytics is not the canonical descriptor.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.288 §6 NWDAF analytics IDs"}, {"id": "question 9516", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per TS 23.122 / TS 24.501, the PLMN selection procedure in the UE includes reporting the available PLMNs to NAS on request - a recognised purpose of PLMN-selection behaviour in the UE.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.122 §4.4; TS 24.501"}, {"id": "question 9664", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "IEEE 802.11a-1999 §17.3.10.1 specifies that CCA / busy-channel determination for the OFDM PHY uses received signal strength thresholds (energy detect / signal detect). It is not based on PLCP header fields, U-NII bandwidth, or generic OFDM characteristics.", "source": "IEEE 802.11a-1999 §17.3.10.1 OFDM CCA"}, {"id": "question 9728", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per TS 23.012 and TR 21.905, location registration exists to keep the network informed of the UE's location so that services (calls/data) can be delivered over the whole system area - i.e. continuity of service across the system area, matching option 4.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.012 (Location Management); TR 21.905"}, {"id": "question 9780", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "In GMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE, the UE performs normal operations including cell selection, sending user data, paging response, signalling and periodic/triggered routing-area updates. Whether option 3 ('not initiate normal RAU') is correct depends on whether 'initiate' means spontaneous or triggered; the wording is not precise enough.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.060 §6.7 GMM; TS 24.008"}, {"id": "question 9831", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "IEEE 802.11-2020 §4 defines the Distribution System as the integration point between APs that enables mobility by allowing frames to be forwarded between APs as a station moves between BSSes - matching option 2 'Providing mobility by connecting access points'.", "source": "IEEE 802.11-2020 §4 (Distribution System)"}, {"id": "question 9834", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "In 5G NR random-access contention resolution (TS 38.321 §5.1.5), the gNB echoes back the UE-generated CCCH SDU (a random value) in the UE Contention Resolution Identity MAC CE; meanwhile, the Temporary C-RNTI addresses the Msg4 PDCCH. Both 'temporary C-RNTI' (option 3) and 'random value for contention resolution' (option 4) are temporary UE identifiers used for contention resolution; the question is underspecified.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.1.5; TS 38.213"}, {"id": "question 9953", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "RSTP port roles (Root, Designated, Alternate, Backup) and states (Forwarding, Discarding) are all assignable. In a network without loops, Root/Designated/Alternate roles and Forwarding states can all be present; the question conflates roles and states, and the marked 'Forwarding is NOT a status' is not unambiguously supported by IEEE 802.1D/Q.", "source": "IEEE 802.1D-2004 / 802.1Q (STP/RSTP roles and states)"}]